{"id": "eafa1eaf-22b8-42a6-9280-3cc145db3be8", "timestamp": "2026-11-17T20:17:37.561663", "source": "environment", "tool_name": "terminal", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ac5f920ec6c98838", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "(1111, 529)"}], "is_error": false, "command": "python -c \"import pandas as pd; df = pd.read_csv('data/validationData.csv'); print(df.shape)\"", "exit_code": 0, "timeout": false, "metadata": {"exit_code": 0, "pid": -1, "username": "user", "hostname": "host", "working_dir": "~/owl-repro-work/P61-20261118T011646Z/openhands-gemma-4-31b-owl/workspace", "py_interpreter_path": "~/owl-repro-envs/ml/bin/python", "prefix": "", "suffix": "\n[The command completed with exit code 0.]"}, "full_output_save_dir": "~/owl-repro-work/P61-20261118T011646Z/openhands-gemma-4-31b-owl/state/.openhands/conversations/f0b8e3ca895d4bed97ff8311fc13503c/observations", "kind": "TerminalObservation"}, "action_id": "81c1565a-07e2-49f1-a5a1-52dc6775c6b0", "kind": "ObservationEvent"}
{"id": "9340205f-ac87-4b4c-8a6e-f244c669b516", "timestamp": "2026-11-17T20:19:38.546041", "source": "agent", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"thought": "The goal is to reproduce the \"building and floor accuracy\" of the WiFi FAB-MAP method on the UJIIndoorLoc validation set.\n\nKey details from the paper and OWL_SPEC.json:\n1.  **Feature Extraction**:\n    - WiFi scans are converted to binary vectors.\n    - Bin width = 5 dBm.\n    - Range: -110 to 0 (or -10 as per paper, but OWL_SPEC says -110 to 0). Let's re-read the paper.\n    - Paper says: \"Thresholds were uniformly distributed in range <-110, -10> with a step (bin width) of 10 dBm.\"\n    - For bin width 5, the range would be -110 to -10 (or 0).\n    - A bin is 1 if the signal strength exceeds the threshold, 0 otherwise.\n    - Let's define the bins: $T_i = -110 + i \\cdot \\text{bin\\_width}$.\n    - For bin\\_width = 5, $T_0 = -110, T_1 = -105, \\dots, T_{19} = -15, T_{20} = -10$.\n    - Wait, if the range is -110 to -10, and bin width is 5, there are $( -10 - (-110) ) / 5 = 20$ bins.\n    - Each WAP signal is compared to these 20 thresholds.\n    - This results in a binary vector of size $520 \\times 20 = 10,400$ dimensions.\n\n2.  **FAB-MAP Algorithm**:\n    - FAB-MAP is essentially a Bayesian approach to place recognition.\n    - It uses a Chow-Liu tree to model the joint probability of features.\n    - The core is estimating $P(Z | L)$, where $Z$ is the feature vector and $L$ is the location.\n    - In FAB-MAP, the features are often treated as independent given the location, but the Chow-Liu tree is used to model the *prior* or the *joint distribution* of features across the environment to handle \"common\" features (those that appear in many places).\n    - Actually, FAB-MAP's key is the \"joint probability distribution of re-observation of a place given a set of features\".\n    - Let's look at the standard FAB-MAP (Cummins & Newman). It uses a Chow-Liu tree to approximate the joint distribution of visual words.\n    - In this WiFi adaptation:\n        - Features are the binary bins.\n        - The Chow-Liu tree is used to estimate the joint probability of these binary features.\n        - The probability of a place $L$ being observed given features $Z$ is $P(L | Z) \\propto P(Z | L) P(L)$.\n        - $P(Z | L)$ is often modeled as a product of independent Bernoulli distributions for each feature $z_i$: $P(Z | L) = \\prod P(z_i | L)$.\n        - However, the \"common\" features are handled by the Chow-Liu tree which models the global distribution $P(Z)$.\n        - The \"re-observation\" probability in FAB-MAP is often $P(Z | L) / P(Z)$.\n        - $P(Z)$ is approximated by the Chow-Liu tree.\n\n3.  **Parameters**:\n    - Bin width = 5.\n    - $PzGe = 0.3135$ (Probability of a feature being present given it's in the environment).\n    - $PzGne = 0.0004$ (Probability of a feature being present given it's NOT in the environment).\n    - These look like the parameters for the \"generative\" model of the features.\n    - $P(z_i=1 | L)$ and $P(z_i=1 | \\neg L)$.\n    - If $z_i$ is present at location $L$, $P(z_i=1 | L) = PzGe$.\n    - If $z_i$ is not present at location $L$, $P(z_i=1 | L) = PzGne$.\n    - Wait, the paper says \"PzGe = 0.3135, PzGne = 0.0004\". This is likely the probability of a feature being \"active\" given it's \"expected\" vs \"not expected\".\n\n4.  **Matching Logic**:\n    - For each test scan $Z_{test}$, we want to find the location $L$ in the database that maximizes $P(L | Z_{test})$.\n    - $P(L | Z_{test}) \\propto P(Z_{test} | L) P(L)$.\n    - $P(Z_{test} | L) = \\prod_{i} P(z_{i, test} | L)$.\n    - If $z_{i, database}$ is 1 (feature present at $L$), then $P(z_{i, test}=1 | L) = PzGe$ and $P(z_{i, test}=0 | L) = 1 - PzGe$.\n    - If $z_{i, database}$ is 0 (feature absent at $L$), then $P(z_{i, test}=1 | L) = PzGne$ and $P(z_{i, test}=0 | L) = 1 - PzGne$.\n    - This is a simple generative model. The \"FAB-MAP\" part usually involves the Chow-Liu tree to model $P(Z)$ to avoid overcounting common features.\n    - But if we are just doing a simple match, maybe the Chow-Liu tree is used to refine the probabilities?\n    - Let's re-read the paper's description of the Chow-Liu tree.\n    - \"our method uses Chow-Liu trees to estimate a joint probability distribution of re-observation of a place given a set of features\".\n    - In the original FAB-MAP, the Chow-Liu tree is used to model the distribution of features across the entire map.\n    - $P(Z | L) = \\frac{P(Z, L)}{P(L)}$.\n    - The \"re-observation\" probability is $P(Z | L) / P(Z)$.\n    - $P(Z)$ is the probability of seeing these features anywhere in the environment.\n    - $P(Z)$ is approximated by the Chow-Liu tree.\n    - So the match score for location $L$ is $\\frac{P(Z_{test} | L)}{P(Z_{test})}$.\n    - Since $P(Z_{test})$ is the same for all $L$, we just need $P(Z_{test} | L)$.\n    - But wait, if $P(Z_{test})$ is just a constant, why use the Chow-Liu tree?\n    - In FAB-MAP, the Chow-Liu tree is used to compute $P(Z)$ for the *current* scan to decide if it's a \"known\" place or \"unknown\".\n    - For the \"building and floor accuracy\", we are likely just matching the test scan to the best location in the database.\n    - If we just need the best $L$, $P(Z_{test} | L)$ is sufficient.\n    - However, the paper mentions the Chow-Liu tree specifically.\n    - Let's look at the \"Target\" in TASK.md: \"Building-and-floor accuracy... of the proposed WiFi FAB-MAP method\".\n    - If the method is just $P(Z_{test} | L)$, it's not really FAB-MAP, it's just a generative match.\n    - Let's check if the Chow-Liu tree is used for something else.\n    - \"The new method is evaluated on the UJIIndoorLoc dataset... allowing a fair comparison with other solutions.\"\n    - Maybe the Chow-Liu tree is used to model the *prior* $P(L)$? No, that's unlikely.\n    - Let's think about $P(Z | L)$ again.\n    - If we use the generative model $P(z_i | L)$ based on $PzGe$ and $PzGne$, we get a score.\n    - Is there any other part of FAB-MAP?\n    - FAB-MAP also uses a \"place\" model.\n    - Let's assume the matching is:\n        $\\text{Score}(L) = \\sum_{i} \\log P(z_{i, test} | L)$\n        where $P(z_{i, test} | L) = PzGe$ if $z_{i, test}=1$ and $z_{i, database}=1$\n        $P(z_{i, test} | L) = 1 - PzGe$ if $z_{i, test}=0$ and $z_{i, database}=1$\n        $P(z_{i, test} | L) = PzGne$ if $z_{i, test}=1$ and $z_{i, database}=0$\n        $P(z_{i, test} | L) = 1 - PzGne$ if $z_{i, test}=0$ and $z_{i, database}=0$\n    - This is the standard \"generative\" match.\n    - Does the Chow-Liu tree change this?\n    - In FAB-MAP, the Chow-Liu tree is used to estimate $P(Z)$.\n    - The probability of a place $L$ is $P(L | Z) \\propto \\frac{P(Z | L)}{P(Z)} P(L)$.\n    - Again, $P(Z)$ is constant for all $L$.\n    - Maybe the Chow-Liu tree is used to estimate $P(Z | L)$ more accurately?\n    - \"our method uses Chow-Liu trees to estimate a joint probability distribution of re-observation of a place given a set of features\".\n    - This phrasing is a bit weird. \"re-observation of a place given a set of features\" usually means $P(L | Z)$.\n    - Let's look at the original FAB-MAP. The Chow-Liu tree is used to model the distribution of features $P(Z)$.\n    - The \"re-observation\" probability is $P(Z | L) / P(Z)$.\n    - If we are just matching, we can ignore $P(Z)$.\n    - But maybe the \"score\" in the paper is not just the best match, but something that uses the Chow-Liu tree to filter out unlikely matches?\n    - The TASK.md says \"Building-and-floor accuracy (score = fraction of examples whose matched database location has both the correct building ID and floor number)\".\n    - This implies we just need the best match $L^* = \\arg\\max_L P(L | Z)$.\n    - If $P(L)$ is uniform, $L^* = \\arg\\max_L P(Z | L)$.\n    - Let's try implementing the generative match first. If the result is close to 0.92, then the Chow-Liu tree might not be critical for the *best* match, only for the *confidence* of the match.\n    - Wait, the paper says \"The decision to test lower values of PzGne was motivated by the fact that in the case of WiFi scans it is rather unlikely to not detect a network that is in the range of a device\".\n    - This confirms the generative model: $PzGne$ is the probability of a \"false positive\" (detecting a WAP that isn't there).\n\n5.  **Implementation Plan**:\n    - **Data Loading**: Load `trainingData.csv` and `validationData.csv`.\n    - **Feature Extraction**:\n        - For each scan, for each WAP:\n            - If RSSI is 100, it's \"not found\".\n            - If RSSI < 100, it's a valid signal.\n            - Binning: For each threshold $T \\in \\{-110, -105, \\dots, -10\\}$, if $RSSI \\ge T$, bin is 1, else 0.\n            - Wait, the paper says \"Thresholds were uniformly distributed in range <-110, -10> with a step (bin width) of 10 dBm.\"\n            - For bin width 5, the thresholds are $-110, -105, \\dots, -10$.\n            - Let's check the number of bins. $(-10 - (-110)) / 5 = 20$.\n            - So for each WAP, we have 20 binary features.\n            - Total features = $520 \\times 20 = 10,400$.\n    - **Database Construction**:\n        - Use the training set as the database of known places.\n        - Wait, the paper says \"The balanced training dataset... was divided into two parts: subtraining and validation.\"\n        - And \"we used a part of the training dataset in the validation procedure, and treated the validation dataset as a test set.\"\n        - This means the \"database of known places\" is built from the \"subtraining\" part of the training set.\n        - But the TASK.md says \"database/Chow-Liu tree built from the training set\".\n        - Let's use the whole `trainingData.csv` as the database for simplicity, or check if there's a specific split.\n        - Actually, the TASK.md says \"database/Chow-Liu tree built from the training set\". I'll use all of `trainingData.csv`.\n    - **Matching**:\n        - For each scan in `validationData.csv`:\n            - Find $L \\in \\text{trainingData.csv}$ that maximizes $\\sum_{i} \\log P(z_{i, test} | L)$.\n            - $P(z_{i, test} | L) = PzGe$ if $z_{i, test}=1$ and $z_{i, database}=1$\n            - $P(z_{i, test} | L) = 1 - PzGe$ if $z_{i, test}=0$ and $z_{i, database}=1$\n            - $P(z_{i, test} | L) = PzGne$ if $z_{i, test}=1$ and $z_{i, database}=0$\n            - $P(z_{i, test} | L) = 1 - PzGne$ if $z_{i, test}=0$ and $z_{i, database}=0$\n    - **Evaluation**:\n        - Count how many test examples have the same (BuildingID, Floor) as their best match in the database.\n        - Accuracy = (Correct / Total) * 100.\n\n6.  **Wait, what about the Chow-Liu tree?**\n    - If the \"WiFi FAB-MAP method\" *requires* the Chow-Liu tree, and the generative match is not enough, I might need to implement it.\n    - The Chow-Liu tree approximates $P(Z)$.\n    - $P(L | Z) \\propto \\frac{P(Z | L)}{P(Z)} P(L)$.\n    - If $P(L)$ is uniform, $P(L | Z) \\propto \\frac{P(Z | L)}{P(Z)}$.\n    - Since $P(Z)$ is the same for all $L$, the $\\arg\\max$ is the same.\n    - The only way the Chow-Liu tree matters for the *best* match is if $P(L)$ is not uniform or if the Chow-Liu tree is used to model $P(Z | L)$ instead of $P(Z)$.\n    - But the standard FAB-MAP uses it for $P(Z)$.\n    - Let's try the generative match first. If it's not enough, I'll implement the Chow-Liu tree to see if it changes anything (though it shouldn't for the $\\arg\\max$).\n\n7.  **Memory Constraints**:\n    - Training data: 19,937 rows, 529 columns.\n    - Binary features: $19,937 \\times 10,400$ bits.\n    - $19,937 \\times 10,400 \\approx 2 \\times 10^8$ bits $\\approx 25$ MB. This is well within 6 GB.\n    - However, if I use `float64` for the binary features, it's $2 \\times 10^8 \\times 8$ bytes $\\approx 1.6$ GB. Still okay.\n    - I should use `np.packbits` or just boolean arrays to save memory.\n\n8.  **Refining the Binning**:\n    - Range: -110 to -10.\n    - Bin width: 5.\n    - Thresholds: $T_j = -110 + 5j$ for $j = 0, 1, \\dots, 20$.\n    - Wait, if the range is -110 to -10, and we have a step of 5, we have thresholds at -110, -105, ..., -10.\n    - That's 21 thresholds.\n    - Let's use $T = \\{-110, -105, \\dots, -10\\}$.\n    - For each WAP, the feature vector is $[RSSI \\ge T_0, RSSI \\ge T_1, \\dots, RSSI \\ge T_{20}]$.\n    - This is 21 features per WAP.\n    - Total features = $520 \\times 21 = 10,920$.\n\n9.  **Final check on $PzGe$ and $PzGne$**:\n    - $PzGe = 0.3135$\n    - $PzGne = 0.0004$\n    - These are the probabilities of a feature being 1.\n    - $P(z=1 | \\text{expected}) = 0.3135$\n    - $P(z=1 | \\text{unexpected}) = 0.0004$\n    - This means if the feature is present in the database, we expect it to be present in the test scan with probability 0.3135.\n    - If it's not in the database, we expect it to be present with probability 0.0004.\n    - This is a very low $PzGe$! Usually, if a feature is present, you'd expect it to be present in the test scan with high probability.\n    - But maybe the \"features\" are very sparse or the environment is very noisy.\n    - Let's follow the numbers provided.\n\n10. **Wait, the \"score\" is 0.92**.\n    - Let's implement this and see.\n\nPlan:\n1.  Implement `reproduce.py`.\n2.  Load data.\n3.  Convert RSSI to binary features using the specified binning.\n4.  Implement the generative matching.\n5.  Calculate building and floor accuracy.\n6.  Write `RESULT.json`.\n", "kind": "ThinkAction"}, "tool_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8a859a1cff29be3f", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-8a859a1cff29be3f", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "think", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Analyze FAB-MAP method and implementation details\", \"thought\": \"The goal is to reproduce the \\\"building and floor accuracy\\\" of the WiFi FAB-MAP method on the UJIIndoorLoc validation set.\\n\\nKey details from the paper and OWL_SPEC.json:\\n1.  **Feature Extraction**:\\n    - WiFi scans are converted to binary vectors.\\n    - Bin width = 5 dBm.\\n    - Range: -110 to 0 (or -10 as per paper, but OWL_SPEC says -110 to 0). Let's re-read the paper.\\n    - Paper says: \\\"Thresholds were uniformly distributed in range <-110, -10> with a step (bin width) of 10 dBm.\\\"\\n    - For bin width 5, the range would be -110 to -10 (or 0).\\n    - A bin is 1 if the signal strength exceeds the threshold, 0 otherwise.\\n    - Let's define the bins: $T_i = -110 + i \\\\cdot \\\\text{bin\\\\_width}$.\\n    - For bin\\\\_width = 5, $T_0 = -110, T_1 = -105, \\\\dots, T_{19} = -15, T_{20} = -10$.\\n    - Wait, if the range is -110 to -10, and bin width is 5, there are $( -10 - (-110) ) / 5 = 20$ bins.\\n    - Each WAP signal is compared to these 20 thresholds.\\n    - This results in a binary vector of size $520 \\\\times 20 = 10,400$ dimensions.\\n\\n2.  **FAB-MAP Algorithm**:\\n    - FAB-MAP is essentially a Bayesian approach to place recognition.\\n    - It uses a Chow-Liu tree to model the joint probability of features.\\n    - The core is estimating $P(Z | L)$, where $Z$ is the feature vector and $L$ is the location.\\n    - In FAB-MAP, the features are often treated as independent given the location, but the Chow-Liu tree is used to model the *prior* or the *joint distribution* of features across the environment to handle \\\"common\\\" features (those that appear in many places).\\n    - Actually, FAB-MAP's key is the \\\"joint probability distribution of re-observation of a place given a set of features\\\".\\n    - Let's look at the standard FAB-MAP (Cummins & Newman). It uses a Chow-Liu tree to approximate the joint distribution of visual words.\\n    - In this WiFi adaptation:\\n        - Features are the binary bins.\\n        - The Chow-Liu tree is used to estimate the joint probability of these binary features.\\n        - The probability of a place $L$ being observed given features $Z$ is $P(L | Z) \\\\propto P(Z | L) P(L)$.\\n        - $P(Z | L)$ is often modeled as a product of independent Bernoulli distributions for each feature $z_i$: $P(Z | L) = \\\\prod P(z_i | L)$.\\n        - However, the \\\"common\\\" features are handled by the Chow-Liu tree which models the global distribution $P(Z)$.\\n        - The \\\"re-observation\\\" probability in FAB-MAP is often $P(Z | L) / P(Z)$.\\n        - $P(Z)$ is approximated by the Chow-Liu tree.\\n\\n3.  **Parameters**:\\n    - Bin width = 5.\\n    - $PzGe = 0.3135$ (Probability of a feature being present given it's in the environment).\\n    - $PzGne = 0.0004$ (Probability of a feature being present given it's NOT in the environment).\\n    - These look like the parameters for the \\\"generative\\\" model of the features.\\n    - $P(z_i=1 | L)$ and $P(z_i=1 | \\\\neg L)$.\\n    - If $z_i$ is present at location $L$, $P(z_i=1 | L) = PzGe$.\\n    - If $z_i$ is not present at location $L$, $P(z_i=1 | L) = PzGne$.\\n    - Wait, the paper says \\\"PzGe = 0.3135, PzGne = 0.0004\\\". This is likely the probability of a feature being \\\"active\\\" given it's \\\"expected\\\" vs \\\"not expected\\\".\\n\\n4.  **Matching Logic**:\\n    - For each test scan $Z_{test}$, we want to find the location $L$ in the database that maximizes $P(L | Z_{test})$.\\n    - $P(L | Z_{test}) \\\\propto P(Z_{test} | L) P(L)$.\\n    - $P(Z_{test} | L) = \\\\prod_{i} P(z_{i, test} | L)$.\\n    - If $z_{i, database}$ is 1 (feature present at $L$), then $P(z_{i, test}=1 | L) = PzGe$ and $P(z_{i, test}=0 | L) = 1 - PzGe$.\\n    - If $z_{i, database}$ is 0 (feature absent at $L$), then $P(z_{i, test}=1 | L) = PzGne$ and $P(z_{i, test}=0 | L) = 1 - PzGne$.\\n    - This is a simple generative model. The \\\"FAB-MAP\\\" part usually involves the Chow-Liu tree to model $P(Z)$ to avoid overcounting common features.\\n    - But if we are just doing a simple match, maybe the Chow-Liu tree is used to refine the probabilities?\\n    - Let's re-read the paper's description of the Chow-Liu tree.\\n    - \\\"our method uses Chow-Liu trees to estimate a joint probability distribution of re-observation of a place given a set of features\\\".\\n    - In the original FAB-MAP, the Chow-Liu tree is used to model the distribution of features across the entire map.\\n    - $P(Z | L) = \\\\frac{P(Z, L)}{P(L)}$.\\n    - The \\\"re-observation\\\" probability is $P(Z | L) / P(Z)$.\\n    - $P(Z)$ is the probability of seeing these features anywhere in the environment.\\n    - $P(Z)$ is approximated by the Chow-Liu tree.\\n    - So the match score for location $L$ is $\\\\frac{P(Z_{test} | L)}{P(Z_{test})}$.\\n    - Since $P(Z_{test})$ is the same for all $L$, we just need $P(Z_{test} | L)$.\\n    - But wait, if $P(Z_{test})$ is just a constant, why use the Chow-Liu tree?\\n    - In FAB-MAP, the Chow-Liu tree is used to compute $P(Z)$ for the *current* scan to decide if it's a \\\"known\\\" place or \\\"unknown\\\".\\n    - For the \\\"building and floor accuracy\\\", we are likely just matching the test scan to the best location in the database.\\n    - If we just need the best $L$, $P(Z_{test} | L)$ is sufficient.\\n    - However, the paper mentions the Chow-Liu tree specifically.\\n    - Let's look at the \\\"Target\\\" in TASK.md: \\\"Building-and-floor accuracy... of the proposed WiFi FAB-MAP method\\\".\\n    - If the method is just $P(Z_{test} | L)$, it's not really FAB-MAP, it's just a generative match.\\n    - Let's check if the Chow-Liu tree is used for something else.\\n    - \\\"The new method is evaluated on the UJIIndoorLoc dataset... allowing a fair comparison with other solutions.\\\"\\n    - Maybe the Chow-Liu tree is used to model the *prior* $P(L)$? No, that's unlikely.\\n    - Let's think about $P(Z | L)$ again.\\n    - If we use the generative model $P(z_i | L)$ based on $PzGe$ and $