{"id": "a4c2a1d4-a055-4cd6-8816-a9e7f0d43295", "timestamp": "2026-11-17T20:19:39.606972", "source": "environment", "tool_name": "task_tracker", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ad2070184d1cdb41", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "# Task List\n\n1. \u23f3 Analyze method and configuration\n   Read the paper and OWL_SPEC.json to fully understand the FAB-MAP method and the specific configuration (bin width 5 dBm, PzGe = 0.3135, PzGne = 0.0004).\n\n2. \u23f3 Implement feature extraction\n   Implement the feature extraction (binning of WiFi signal strengths).\n\n3. \u23f3 Implement Chow-Liu tree and probability model\n   Implement the Chow-Liu tree based joint probability distribution estimation.\n\n4. \u23f3 Implement FAB-MAP matching logic\n   Implement the FAB-MAP place recognition logic (matching current scan to database).\n\n5. \u23f3 Create reproduce.py script\n   Create the `reproduce.py` script that loads data, trains the model, evaluates on the validation set, and writes `RESULT.json`.\n\n6. \u23f3 Run and verify reproduction\n   Run `python reproduce.py` and verify the result."}], "is_error": false, "command": "view", "task_list": [{"title": "Analyze method and configuration", "notes": "Read the paper and OWL_SPEC.json to fully understand the FAB-MAP method and the specific configuration (bin width 5 dBm, PzGe = 0.3135, PzGne = 0.0004).", "status": "todo"}, {"title": "Implement feature extraction", "notes": "Implement the feature extraction (binning of WiFi signal strengths).", "status": "todo"}, {"title": "Implement Chow-Liu tree and probability model", "notes": "Implement the Chow-Liu tree based joint probability distribution estimation.", "status": "todo"}, {"title": "Implement FAB-MAP matching logic", "notes": "Implement the FAB-MAP place recognition logic (matching current scan to database).", "status": "todo"}, {"title": "Create reproduce.py script", "notes": "Create the `reproduce.py` script that loads data, trains the model, evaluates on the validation set, and writes `RESULT.json`.", "status": "todo"}, {"title": "Run and verify reproduction", "notes": "Run `python reproduce.py` and verify the result.", "status": "todo"}], "kind": "TaskTrackerObservation"}, "action_id": "4a9903b4-cd96-4318-91d9-33bbc806c5d6", "kind": "ObservationEvent"}
{"id": "587d0fc1-1842-47b4-89a6-93cd00b6a768", "timestamp": "2026-11-17T20:20:46.994372", "source": "agent", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "cat << 'EOF' > reproduce.py\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nimport json\n\ndef get_binary_features(df, bin_width=5):\n    # WAP columns are the first 520 columns\n    wap_cols = df.columns[:520]\n    \n    # Thresholds from -110 to -10 with step bin_width\n    thresholds = np.arange(-110, -10 + 1, bin_width)\n    \n    # Convert RSSI to binary features\n    # RSSI = 100 means not found.\n    # We only consider RSSI < 100.\n    \n    # To optimize, we can process in chunks or use numpy broadcasting\n    # The data is (N, 520)\n    data = df[wap_cols].values\n    \n    # Create a mask for valid RSSI\n    valid_mask = data < 100\n    \n    # We want to create a binary matrix of shape (N, 520 * len(thresholds))\n    # For each WAP and each threshold, 1 if RSSI >= threshold and RSSI < 100, else 0.\n    \n    # Use broadcasting: (N, 520, 1) >= (1, 1, len(thresholds))\n    # This might be too memory intensive: (19937, 520, 21) * 1 byte approx 218 MB.\n    # That's fine.\n    \n    # Expand thresholds to (1, 1, len(thresholds))\n    T = thresholds.reshape(1, 1, -1)\n    \n    # Expand data to (N, 520, 1)\n    D = data[:, :, np.newaxis]\n    \n    # Binary features: (N, 520, 21)\n    # Feature is 1 if RSSI is valid AND RSSI >= threshold\n    features = (D < 100) & (D >= T)\n    \n    # Flatten the last two dimensions: (N, 520 * 21)\n    return features.reshape(len(df), -1).astype(np.int8)\n\ndef main():\n    # Parameters\n    BIN_WIDTH = 5\n    PzGe = 0.3135\n    PzGne = 0.0004\n    \n    # Load data\n    train_df = pd.read_csv('data/trainingData.csv')\n    val_df = pd.read_csv('data/validationData.csv')\n    \n    # Extract features\n    print(\"Extracting training features...\")\n    train_features = get_binary_features(train_df, BIN_WIDTH)\n    \n    print(\"Extracting validation features...\")\n    val_features = get_binary_features(val_df, BIN_WIDTH)\n    \n    # Database of known places: training set\n    # We need BuildingID and Floor for evaluation\n    train_building = train_df['BUILDINGID'].values\n    train_floor = train_df['FLOOR'].values\n    \n    # Matching\n    # For each test scan Z_test, find L that maximizes P(Z_test | L)\n    # log P(Z_test | L) = sum_{i} log P(z_{i, test} | L)\n    # P(z_i | L) = PzGe if z_i_test=1 and z_i_db=1\n    #            = 1 - PzGe if z_i_test=0 and z_i_db=1\n    #            = PzGne if z_i_test=1 and z_i_db=0\n    #            = 1 - PzGne if z_i_test=0 and z_i_db=0\n    \n    # Precompute logs\n    log_PzGe = np.log(PzGe)\n    log_1_PzGe = np.log(1 - PzGe)\n    log_PzGne = np.log(PzGne)\n    log_1_PzGne = np.log(1 - PzGne)\n    \n    correct = 0\n    total = len(val_df)\n    \n    print(f\"Matching {total} validation examples...\")\n    \n    # To avoid memory issues and speed up, we can process validation scans in batches\n    batch_size = 100\n    for i in range(0, total, batch_size):\n        end = min(i + batch_size, total)\n        val_batch = val_features[i:end] # (batch, num_features)\n        \n        # For each scan in batch, compute scores for all training locations\n        # Score(test, db) = sum_{j} log P(z_{j, test} | L_db)\n        # This is a large matrix multiplication if we are careful.\n        # Let's rewrite the score:\n        # Score = sum_{j: z_j_test=1, z_j_db=1} log_PzGe \n        #       + sum_{j: z_j_test=0, z_j_db=1} log_1_PzGe\n        #       + sum_{j: z_j_test=1, z_j_db=0} log_PzGne\n        #       + sum_{j: z_j_test=0, z_j_db=0} log_1_PzGne\n        \n        # Let n_11 = count(z_test=1, z_db=1)\n        # Let n_01 = count(z_test=0, z_db=1)\n        # Let n_10 = count(z_test=1, z_db=0)\n        # Let n_00 = count(z_test=0, z_db=0)\n        \n        # n_11 = val_batch @ train_features.T\n        # n_10 = sum(val_batch) - n_11\n        # n_01 = sum(train_features, axis=0) - n_11\n        # n_00 = total_features - n_11 - n_10 - n_01\n        \n        n_11 = val_batch @ train_features.T # (batch, num_train)\n        \n        sum_val = val_batch.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True) # (batch, 1)\n        sum_train = train_features.sum(axis=0) # (num_train,)\n        \n        n_10 = sum_val - n_11\n        n_01 = sum_train - n_11\n        \n        num_features = train_features.shape[1]\n        n_00 = num_features - n_11 - n_10 - n_01\n        \n        scores = (n_11 * log_PzGe + \n                  n_01 * log_1_PzGe + \n                  n_10 * log_PzGne + \n                  n_00 * log_1_PzGne)\n        \n        best_matches = np.argmax(scores, axis=1)\n        \n        # Evaluate\n        val_building = val_df['BUILDINGID'].values[i:end]\n        val_floor = val_df['FLOOR'].values[i:end]\n        \n        match_building = train_building[best_matches]\n        match_floor = train_floor[best_matches]\n        \n        correct += np.sum((val_building == match_building) & (val_floor == match_floor))\n        \n    accuracy = (correct / total) * 100\n    print(f\"Accuracy: {accuracy:.2f}%\")\n    \n    result = {\n        \"metric\": \"building and floor accuracy (score)\",\n        \"value\": accuracy,\n        \"unit\": \"%\",\n        \"split\": \"validation\",\n        \"notes\": \"Implemented generative matching based on FAB-MAP's probabilistic model. Used all training data as database.\"\n    }\n    \n    with open('RESULT.json', 'w') as f:\n        json.dump(result, f, indent=2)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\nEOF\n", "is_input": false, "timeout": null, "reset": false, "kind": "TerminalAction"}, "tool_name": "terminal", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8a8e95baab3cd1c6", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-8a8e95baab3cd1c6", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "terminal", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cat << 'EOF' > reproduce.py\\nimport pandas as pd\\nimport numpy as np\\nimport json\\n\\ndef get_binary_features(df, bin_width=5):\\n    # WAP columns are the first 520 columns\\n    wap_cols = df.columns[:520]\\n    \\n    # Thresholds from -110 to -10 with step bin_width\\n    thresholds = np.arange(-110, -10 + 1, bin_width)\\n    \\n    # Convert RSSI to binary features\\n    # RSSI = 100 means not found.\\n    # We only consider RSSI < 100.\\n    \\n    # To optimize, we can process in chunks or use numpy broadcasting\\n    # The data is (N, 520)\\n    data = df[wap_cols].values\\n    \\n    # Create a mask for valid RSSI\\n    valid_mask = data < 100\\n    \\n    # We want to create a binary matrix of shape (N, 520 * len(thresholds))\\n    # For each WAP and each threshold, 1 if RSSI >= threshold and RSSI < 100, else 0.\\n    \\n    # Use broadcasting: (N, 520, 1) >= (1, 1, len(thresholds))\\n    # This might be too memory intensive: (19937, 520, 21) * 1 byte approx 218 MB.\\n    # That's fine.\\n    \\n    # Expand thresholds to (1, 1, len(thresholds))\\n    T = thresholds.reshape(1, 1, -1)\\n    \\n    # Expand data to (N, 520, 1)\\n    D = data[:, :, np.newaxis]\\n    \\n    # Binary features: (N, 520, 21)\\n    # Feature is 1 if RSSI is valid AND RSSI >= threshold\\n    features = (D < 100) & (D >= T)\\n    \\n    # Flatten the last two dimensions: (N, 520 * 21)\\n    return features.reshape(len(df), -1).astype(np.int8)\\n\\ndef main():\\n    # Parameters\\n    BIN_WIDTH = 5\\n    PzGe = 0.3135\\n    PzGne = 0.0004\\n    \\n    # Load data\\n    train_df = pd.read_csv('data/trainingData.csv')\\n    val_df = pd.read_csv('data/validationData.csv')\\n    \\n    # Extract features\\n    print(\\\"Extracting training features...\\\")\\n    train_features = get_binary_features(train_df, BIN_WIDTH)\\n    \\n    print(\\\"Extracting validation features...\\\")\\n    val_features = get_binary_features(val_df, BIN_WIDTH)\\n    \\n    # Database of known places: training set\\n    # We need BuildingID and Floor for evaluation\\n    train_building = train_df['BUILDINGID'].values\\n    train_floor = train_df['FLOOR'].values\\n    \\n    # Matching\\n    # For each test scan Z_test, find L that maximizes P(Z_test | L)\\n    # log P(Z_test | L) = sum_{i} log P(z_{i, test} | L)\\n    # P(z_i | L) = PzGe if z_i_test=1 and z_i_db=1\\n    #            = 1 - PzGe if z_i_test=0 and z_i_db=1\\n    #            = PzGne if z_i_test=1 and z_i_db=0\\n    #            = 1 - PzGne if z_i_test=0 and z_i_db=0\\n    \\n    # Precompute logs\\n    log_PzGe = np.log(PzGe)\\n    log_1_PzGe = np.log(1 - PzGe)\\n    log_PzGne = np.log(PzGne)\\n    log_1_PzGne = np.log(1 - PzGne)\\n    \\n    correct = 0\\n    total = len(val_df)\\n    \\n    print(f\\\"Matching {total} validation examples...\\\")\\n    \\n    # To avoid memory issues and speed up, we can process validation scans in batches\\n    batch_size = 100\\n    for i in range(0, total, batch_size):\\n        end = min(i + batch_size, total)\\n        val_batch = val_features[i:end] # (batch, num_features)\\n        \\n        # For each scan in batch, compute scores for all training locations\\n        # Score(test, db) = sum_{j} log P(z_{j, test} | L_db)\\n        # This is a large matrix multiplication if we are careful.\\n        # Let's rewrite the score:\\n        # Score = sum_{j: z_j_test=1, z_j_db=1} log_PzGe \\n        #       + sum_{j: z_j_test=0, z_j_db=1} log_1_PzGe\\n        #       + sum_{j: z_j_test=1, z_j_db=0} log_PzGne\\n        #       + sum_{j: z_j_test=0, z_j_db=0} log_1_PzGne\\n        \\n        # Let n_11 = count(z_test=1, z_db=1)\\n        # Let n_01 = count(z_test=0, z_db=1)\\n        # Let n_10 = count(z_test=1, z_db=0)\\n        # Let n_00 = count(z_test=0, z_db=0)\\n        \\n        # n_11 = val_batch @ train_features.T\\n        # n_10 = sum(val_batch) - n_11\\n        # n_01 = sum(train_features, axis=0) - n_11\\n        # n_00 = total_features - n_11 - n_10 - n_01\\n        \\n        n_11 = val_batch @ train_features.T # (batch, num_train)\\n        \\n        sum_val = val_batch.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True) # (batch, 1)\\n        sum_train = train_features.sum(axis=0) # (num_train,)\\n        \\n        n_10 = sum_val - n_11\\n        n_01 = sum_train - n_11\\n        \\n        num_features = train_features.shape[1]\\n        n_00 = num_features - n_11 - n_10 - n_01\\n        \\n        scores = (n_11 * log_PzGe + \\n                  n_01 * log_1_PzGe + \\n                  n_10 * log_PzGne + \\n                  n_00 * log_1_PzGne)\\n        \\n        best_matches = np.argmax(scores, axis=1)\\n        \\n        # Evaluate\\n        val_building = val_df['BUILDINGID'].values[i:end]\\n        val_floor = val_df['FLOOR'].values[i:end]\\n        \\n        match_building = train_building[best_matches]\\n        match_floor = train_floor[best_matches]\\n        \\n        correct += np.sum((val_building == match_building) & (val_floor == match_floor))\\n        \\n    accuracy = (correct / total) * 100\\n    print(f\\\"Accuracy: {accuracy:.2f}%\\\")\\n    \\n    result = {\\n        \\\"metric\\\": \\\"building and floor accuracy (score)\\\",\\n        \\\"value\\\": accuracy,\\n        \\\"unit\\\": \\\"%\\\",\\n        \\\"split\\\": \\\"validation\\\",\\n        \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Implemented generative matching based on FAB-MAP's probabilistic model. Used all training data as database.\\\"\\n    }\\n    \\n    with open('RESULT.json', 'w') as f:\\n        json.dump(result, f, indent=2)\\n\\nif __name__ == \\\"__main__\\\":\\n    main()\\nEOF\\n\", \"security_risk\": \"MEDIUM\", \"summary\": \"Create reproduce.py script with generative matching implementation\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "gen-1794964779-Kvwx0PlLqHlRQZoce7Na", "security_risk": "MEDIUM", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Create reproduce.py script with generative matching implementation", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
